course: dictionary
lesson: 14782
title: Thinking ahead with prudent
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prudent is an adjective, so it describes a person, a choice or an action. It means careful and sensible: you think ahead so that you avoid danger or waste. «It would be prudent to bring a coat, just in case the sky turns grey.» The speaker is not saying it will rain for sure. A coat is simply the sensible choice.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. The words «just in case» show that someone is planning for something that might happen. If you swap in a word like silly or careless, the sentence stops making sense, so prudent must mean something like sensible.
explain: Prudent can also be about money. «Mia was prudent with her pocket money and saved half of it each week.» Here prudent means saving and not spending carelessly. The sentence tells you what Mia did, and that is your clue.
fact: Prudent means careful and sensible, thinking ahead so you avoid danger or waste.
fact: Prudent is an adjective, so you can say a prudent choice or it is prudent to wait.
fact: The forms are prudent, more prudent and most prudent.
fact: «It would be prudent to bring a coat.» means it is a sensible thing to do, just in case.
fact: With money, prudent means saving and not spending carelessly. This is a more formal use.
exercise: «It would be prudent to bring a coat.» What does prudent mean here? | Wet and cold | *Careful and sensible, thinking ahead | Bored of the weather | Loud and cheerful
exercise: «Mia was prudent with her pocket money and saved half of it.» What does prudent mean with money? | Losing it on purpose | *Saving and not spending carelessly | Spending all of it at once | Giving it all away
exercise: Which word is the opposite of prudent? | *Reckless | Sensible | Clever | Careful
test: «The prudent climber checked the ropes twice before she climbed.» What does prudent mean? | Happy and loud | Quick and careless | *Careful, thinking ahead to stay safe | Tired and hungry
test: Which of these is a prudent choice? | *Taking an umbrella on a cloudy day | Eating all the cake before tea | Running into a busy road without looking | Leaving the door open in a storm
exam: Which word best replaces prudent in «It is prudent to check the weather before a trip»? | *sensible | loud | silly | lazy
